WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.140
It is a heartbreaking reality that in the United States, drowning is the leading cause

00:00:05.140 --> 00:00:08.400
of death for children ages 1 to 4.

00:00:08.400 --> 00:00:12.720
Drowning is a major emergency that happens when someone's mouth and nose are underwater

00:00:12.720 --> 00:00:15.880
for too long, preventing them from breathing.

00:00:15.880 --> 00:00:19.780
Spotting the signs of drowning early is vital because it often doesn't look like the splashing

00:00:19.780 --> 00:00:21.320
you see in movies.

00:00:21.320 --> 00:00:25.559
Look for someone with their head low in the water, their mouth at water level, or someone

00:00:25.559 --> 00:00:28.120
who is vertical but not using their legs.

00:00:28.120 --> 00:00:32.040
They might be gasping, hyperventilating, or flailing their arms.

00:00:32.040 --> 00:00:34.279
Your first priority is safety.

00:00:34.279 --> 00:00:36.759
Never enter the water if it puts you at risk.

00:00:36.759 --> 00:00:41.480
Instead, try to reach out to them with a pole or throw a flotation device.

00:00:41.480 --> 00:00:45.759
Once the person is out of the water, call 911 immediately and send someone to get an

00:00:45.759 --> 00:00:47.080
AED.

00:00:47.080 --> 00:00:51.799
If the person is unresponsive and not breathing normally, you must start CPR.

00:00:51.799 --> 00:00:56.520
When starting CPR on a drowning victim, you should always start with 5 rescue breaths

00:00:56.520 --> 00:01:01.119
before moving into the standard cycle of 30 compressions and 2 breaths.

00:01:01.119 --> 00:01:04.120
Remember, drowning victims need oxygen first.

00:01:04.120 --> 00:01:08.239
Focus entirely on providing oxygen and maintaining circulation.

00:01:08.239 --> 00:01:12.879
Do not attempt to drain the lungs with abdominal thrusts, as this is ineffective and can cause

00:01:12.879 --> 00:01:13.720
further harm.

